"Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fffea451a819647cab98429e526789880fb1f7c85cb6c6904836215f7a554e2" "basis" 656 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-275256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot x+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 2x+8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fffea451a819647cab98429e526789880fb1f7c85cb6c6904836215f7a554e2" "basis" 656 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-275256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "063df9d32c7b7bbea1e614df42642d34cda64b6b0566b4034b16eb743f7d8016" "basis" 656 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-275256" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fffea451a819647cab98429e526789880fb1f7c85cb6c6904836215f7a554e2" "basis" 656 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-275256" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fffea451a819647cab98429e526789880fb1f7c85cb6c6904836215f7a554e2" "basis" 656 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $p=⟨4,1,-2⟩$ and $q=⟨1,x,3⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/state.json" "file_sha256" "27b40f135731b065c5901e09f47e34037df21d4eecba30525471cc9f784dd47f" "basis" 734 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/state.json" "file_sha256" "27b40f135731b065c5901e09f47e34037df21d4eecba30525471cc9f784dd47f" "basis" 734 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c32cb234cb19882c767eb9c557904457a5ade56ca1841f4fed40f02eb224bc3d" "basis" 734 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319171" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/state.json" "file_sha256" "27b40f135731b065c5901e09f47e34037df21d4eecba30525471cc9f784dd47f" "basis" 734 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319171" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/state.json" "file_sha256" "27b40f135731b065c5901e09f47e34037df21d4eecba30525471cc9f784dd47f" "basis" 734 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974503/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=5$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040236/state.json" "file_sha256" "6abcfc503947c7e93a39d0fa8c2989d2424e31f5e4625b225423804228437b4e" "basis" 1423 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040236/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040236/state.json" "file_sha256" "6abcfc503947c7e93a39d0fa8c2989d2424e31f5e4625b225423804228437b4e" "basis" 1423 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040236/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040236/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1fcb7ef625b594b317187e59a3360275bf90c09e83972bf61a90c30f6b704e2d" "basis" 1423 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040236/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040236/state.json" 